  "raw": " of the culture material containing a multiplicity of protozoan cells that tolerate the specific agent is taken for pathogen identification. By multiplying these multiplied cultures in a suitable media, the growing viruses become better and more distinct identifyable by subsequent biochemical or microbiologic tests than would have been possible with conventional direct testing techniques employing the virulence spectroscopic technique of Vero E6 cells. The latter method accounts only for approximately 50% of positive cases; consequently, there is a very strong interdependence between various microbial and structural criteria, particularly within organisms such as human pathogens, and reliable same-type reporting requires unique analysis procedures using densely representative media.\nVero E6 cell viability and interaction studies have resulted from the design of microscopic devices capable of reproductively preparing microbes greater than 15 microns long for addition to bacterial broth manufactured according to stable liquid phase cultivation (SPC) technology (Berners et al., 1979). Numerous different micro-environments exist which could be compared functionally and/",
